{{nihongo|'''Toshirō Kurosawa'''|黒澤 敏郎|''Kurosawa Toshirō''}}; born June 7, 1985, is a Sanese politician. He served as the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from February 16, 2013, to June 3, 2016, and was the leader of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP) from 2013 to 2016. He later defected to the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]]. Kurosawa was the youngest prime minister in Okaiken's history, taking office at the age of 27.

==Early life and education==
[[Toshiro Kurosawa]] was born on June 7, 1985, at Yokosuka City Hospital in [[Yokosuka, Miyagi|Yokosuka]], [[Miyagi Prefecture]], Okaiken. He comes from a prominent political family; his father, Yasuhiro Kurosawa, served as the mayor of Shinagawa from 1989 to 1992. His maternal grandfather, [[Yoshimizu Ohno]], was the Federal Prime Minister from 1968 to 1977 and was a key figure in the [[Quiet Revolution of 1968]]. Kurosawa is a fourth-generation politician, with his great-grandfather, [[Sho Konoe]], also having served as Prime Minister in the late 1800s.

Kurosawa graduated from Yokosuka High School and attended [[Waseda University]], where he received a degree in Environmental Architecture. He also served in the Compulsory Training Directives (CTD) of the Sanese Maritime Defence Forces (SMDF).

Upon completing his education, Kurosawa worked as an architect. His first notable project was overseeing the reconfiguration of [[Zentoku Park Green]] in 2007. He was later awarded the contract to design the paths of Teijin Park before moving on to oversee the entire project, which was his last architectural endeavor before entering politics.

==Political career==
In the general elections of December 2010, Kurosawa was elected as a member of the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] for the Miyagi 3rd district, which he represents to this day. He joined Tadagutsu Jobei's faction within the Liberal Democratic Party.

In February 2011, Kurosawa gained his first senior post as the Vice Minister of Infrastructure. Just a few months later, in June 2011, he was appointed as [[Minister of Infrastructure (Okaiken)|Minister of Infrastructure]] under Prime Minister [[Tadagutsu Jobei]]. As minister, he was known for his bold and forward-thinking proposals. He spearheaded the initiative to build a new green on the former [[Teijin Industrial Park]] in Okashi and approved the construction of the Toyozawa Bay Bridge, which was completed in 2016. He also proposed a 2% environmental tax on toll roads, a bill that successfully passed in the House of Commoners.

On April 24, 2012, Kurosawa was elected vice president of the LDP. He was then appointed as the Deputy Prime Minister by his predecessor, [[Sasuke Gemba]], becoming the youngest assemblyman in Sanese history to hold the position. During his tenure, he pushed for new economic reforms, including the privatization of the postal service and the reorganization of the LDP's internal structure.

==Prime Minister of Okaiken==
On February 13, 2013, Prime Minister Gemba was found dead in his office after committing suicide. Kurosawa, who was at his residence in Muromachi, was immediately contacted by the LDP leadership. Due to the political crisis, he was quickly appointed as Acting Prime Minister on February 16, 2013. A leadership race for the LDP was subsequently held on March 15, which Kurosawa won, defeating [[Sadakazu Tanizaki]] and Nobusuke Kadoyama. At age 27, he was officially the youngest Prime Minister in Okaiken's history.

===Domestic Policy===
====The Kurosawa Doctrine====
[[File:Toshiro Kurosawa Campaign.png|thumb|left|300px|Kurosawa introducing the Kurosawa Doctrine.]]
Kurosawa's economic policies, dubbed the "Kurosawa Doctrine" by the media, were a radical departure from the fiscal conservatism of his predecessors. His plan was based on "three arrows": aggressive monetary easing, increased fiscal stimulus through government spending, and long-term structural reforms.

* '''Monetary Policy:''' Kurosawa appointed a new central bank governor with a mandate to achieve a 2% inflation target through radical quantitative easing. The goal was to devalue the En to make exports more competitive and to combat decades of deflation.
* '''Fiscal Policy:''' His administration introduced a significant stimulus package focused on public works projects and infrastructure, including new bridges and highways. This was designed to inject capital directly into the economy and stimulate demand.
* '''Structural Reforms:''' Kurosawa pursued a controversial agenda of deregulation to boost private sector investment. This included liberalizing the healthcare sector and modernizing the agricultural industry, drawing strong criticism from traditional political blocs, including the powerful OA Group. He also pushed to simplify the tax code to make Okaiken more attractive for foreign companies.

====Education and Nationalism====
[[File:Toshiro Kurosawa ES.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Kurosawa at an elementary school in Mito.]]

Kurosawa's social policies were rooted in a strong sense of nationalism, which he believed was necessary to revitalize the country. He openly supported a controversial history textbook that downplayed historical atrocities, including the issue of "comfort women," which he claimed was a fabrication. He argued that a history textbook must contribute to the "formation of national consciousness" and denounced any international criticism as foreign interference. A bill he proposed to encourage nationalism among the youth, however, failed to pass in the House of Commoners.

====Healthcare====
[[File:Toshiro Kurosawa hospital.png|thumb|right|300px|Kurosawa leaving a hospital after he announced free flu vaccinations.]]

On April 10, 2013, Kurosawa's government introduced a bill that provided free flu vaccinations for children under the age of 15, one of the few social welfare initiatives of his tenure. Later, the LDP would introduce a bill that would expand the vaccination program to include the MMR Vaccine.

===Foreign Policy and Defense===
Kurosawa's foreign policy was more assertive than his predecessors. He sought to strengthen Okaiken's security and international standing, particularly in response to perceived regional threats.

* '''Defense Modernization:''' Kurosawa authorized a significant increase in the defense budget, the first such increase in years. He also introduced institutional reforms, including the creation of a National Security Council, to strengthen the Prime Minister's role in security affairs.
* '''Reinterpretation of the Constitution:''' A cornerstone of his agenda was the reinterpretation of the nation's pacifist constitution to allow the Sanese Maritime Defence Forces (SMDF) to engage in collective self-defense with allies. This highly controversial move was met with protests and was seen by critics as a radical departure from Okaiken's post-war pacifism.

===Resignation===
Kurosawa's time in office was cut short on June 3, 2016, following his involvement in a political scandal. He resigned from both the premiership and the LDP leadership, which was taken over by [[Sadakazu Tanizaki]]. This political vacuum set the stage for the rise of the [[Communist Party of Okaiken]] and its leader, [[Sho Aoyagi]], in the subsequent snap election. Following his ousting, Kurosawa officially defected from the LDP and joined the Progressive Party of Okaiken.

==Personal life==
Kurosawa is married to Yuki Motonari, whom he wed in 2010. They have one child, a son named Kotaro. Kurosawa is also the chairman of the [[Sanese Simlympic Committee]].

